<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/intermediate/what-buildings-must-do">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every building, from a hut to a hospital, answers the same brief: <strong>stand up, serve
    its purpose, and be worth inhabiting</strong> — Vitruvius's firmitas, utilitas, venustas,
    2,000 years old and still the profession's checklist. Our century added a fourth: do it
    without wrecking the climate. What makes this checklist durable is that each leg is a
    different <em>kind</em> of test — firmitas is physics and code, utilitas is logistics and
    physiology, venustas is perception and culture, and the fourth leg is thermodynamics and
    carbon accounting. A building can pass any three and fail the fourth; the architect's
    job is holding all of them at once, under budget, on a site they did not choose. This
    page walks each leg and then widens the lens from room to city, because the built
    environment's health effects do not respect the property line.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three classical legs plus the modern fourth" widget="equationStep" config={vitruvCfg} />

  <h2>Firmitas: standing up, and staying safe when things go wrong</h2>
  <p>
    The structural leg is the engineering lessons' territory — load paths, beams, buckling —
    but in buildings it extends beyond gravity. A building must also survive what the site
    throws at it: wind (which scales with height and, on tall towers, becomes the design
    load), snow, earthquakes (where ductility — the ability to bend without breaking —
    matters more than raw strength), and fire. Fire safety is its own discipline:
    <strong>compartmentation</strong> (keep fire in one zone), <strong>escape design</strong>
    (every occupant reaches a protected stair within a calculable travel time),
    <strong>material behaviour</strong> (steel loses half its strength around 550–600 °C,
    hence fireproofing coatings). History is the evidence base: the Great Fire of London
    (1666) produced the first modern building act — brick replacing timber, party walls
    thickening — and the codes that followed are, in effect, a written record of every way
    buildings have failed. The modern cautionary case is Grenfell Tower (2017): a
    combustible cladding system on an occupied high-rise, 72 dead, and a regulatory
    system that had allowed the <em>appearance</em> of compliance to drift from the
    substance. Firmitas is not a style; it is the non-negotiable leg.
  </p>

  <h2>Utilitas: the building as machine for its program</h2>
  <p>
    Function begins with the <strong>brief</strong> (the programme): the room-by-room
    schedule of what the building must contain and how its inhabitants move. Clients
    usually specify solutions ("we want a 200-seat hall"); architects must recover the
    needs beneath ("people need to assemble, hear, and leave in five minutes" — which a
    different, better room might serve). That translation is design work, and it is why
    interviewing and observation belong to architecture as much as to UX research. Then
    the building must <em>perform</em>: a hospital is an air-handling machine with wards
    attached (operating theatres run positive pressure so germs flow out; infectious
    wards run negative so they flow in), a theatre is an acoustics and sightline instrument
    (the raked floor keeps every eye's ray unblocked), a kitchen-to-dining-room path must
    never cross the dirty-dishes return, an office is a circulation and encounter
    diagram. <strong>Circulation</strong> — how people actually move through the plan —
    decides whether a building feels effortless or exhausting; the corridor is the
    building's most-used room, and the one nobody budgets for.
  </p>

  <h2>Shelter: the climate machine in the walls</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest utilitas is still the biggest: keeping the inside comfortable when the
    outside is not. <strong>Passive design</strong> does this with geometry before
    machinery: orient glazing toward winter sun and shade it from summer's high angle
    (overhangs tuned to latitude do this for free), insulate to slow conduction (the
    thermodynamics lesson's Fourier arithmetic, priced per wall), seal against uncontrolled
    air leakage, and ventilate deliberately. The numbers are large: in a temperate
    climate, a well-oriented, well-insulated house can cut heating demand by 70–90%
    against a typical 20th-century stock — the Passivhaus standard (space-heating demand
    under 15 kWh/m²·year, roughly a tenth of conventional) is passive design made
    quantitative. Ventilation has a measured cognitive price when neglected: occupied,
    poorly ventilated rooms drift past ~1,000 ppm of CO<sub>2</sub> within an hour
    (each person exhales about 5 litres a minute), and studies of classrooms and offices
    link such concentrations to measurably slower, less accurate decision-making. The
    stuffy classroom is not an aesthetic complaint; it is an air-changes-per-hour failure
    with exam scores attached.
  </p>

  <h2>Health, safety, welfare: the measurable shadows of design</h2>
  <p>
    The built environment acts on bodies in ways that can be — and now are — measured.
    <strong>Daylight and views:</strong> Roger Ulrich's 1984 hospital study found
    gallbladder-surgery patients whose beds faced trees needed fewer strong analgesics
    and were discharged sooner than matched patients facing a brick wall — the founding
    result of evidence-based design, since replicated across wards, schools and offices.
    Daylight also entrains circadian rhythm; windowless interiors measurably disturb
    sleep. <strong>Noise:</strong> chronic exposure above ~55 dB is associated with
    hypertension and learning deficits — which is why school siting treats traffic as a
    clinical variable. <strong>Sanitation:</strong> the city-scale case study is cholera:
    John Snow's 1854 mapping of deaths around the Broad Street pump founded epidemiology,
    and Bazalgette's London sewers (1860s) built the lesson in brick — public health's
    greatest infrastructure was urban design. The same lens covers today's debates:
    dense cities with walkable streets, parks and transit show lower traffic mortality
    and better cardiovascular outcomes than car-dependent sprawl; a "15-minute city"
    is a health intervention wearing a planning vocabulary.
  </p>

  <h2>Venustas: delight is a load-bearing word</h2>
  <p>
    The third leg resists the spreadsheet — and survives on evidence of a different kind.
    Delight is the felt quality of being somewhere: proportion (spaces whose dimensions
    relate legibly), light (direct sun for joy, filtered light for calm, shadow for
    depth), material (the temperature of stone under a palm; timber's acoustics), and the
    <em>figure-ground</em> of space itself — enclosure, prospect, refuge. The evidence
    that this leg is functional rather than decorative: people maintain, respect and
    revisit buildings they find beautiful; brutalist estates that failed socially often
    failed where their public spaces felt illegible or unsafe (Oscar Newman's
    <em>defensible space</em> work, 1972, tied layout legibility to vandalism and crime
    rates); and "loved" buildings get adapted rather than demolished — longevity is the
    greenest quality a building can have. Venustas is also cultural, not universal:
    proportion systems (the golden section, the classical orders, Japanese
    <em>ma</em>) are traditions of attention, and part of an architect's education is
    learning several fluently rather than worshipping one.
  </p>

  <h2>The fourth leg: energy and carbon</h2>
  <p>
    Buildings and construction account for roughly a third of global energy-related
    CO<sub>2</sub> emissions: building <em>operations</em> (heating, cooling, lighting,
    hot water) consume about 30% of world final energy, and the <strong>embodied
    carbon</strong> of materials adds the rest — cement alone is ~7–8% of global
    CO<sub>2</sub>, steel similar. The engineering implication is a two-front war:
    operational carbon (insulation, airtightness, heat pumps — the thermodynamics and
    heat-transfer lessons applied to an envelope) and embodied carbon (timber where
    steel will do, reclaimed structure, cement substitutes). The uncomfortable
    arithmetic: a new, efficient building can emit more carbon on construction day
    than it saves annually for a decade — which is why the profession's current
    mantra is <strong>"the greenest building is the one already standing"</strong>,
    and retrofits carry more climate leverage than most showpieces.
  </p>

  <h2>Codes, briefs and sites: the working constraints</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Building codes</strong> (fire, structure, accessibility, energy, drainage)
    are the enforceable floor — the minimum health-and-safety bargain society writes
    after its failures. They are the floor, not the ceiling: architecture happens above
    them, and "it complies" is praise from no one. <strong>Sites</strong> constrain and
    generate: orientation and sun path, wind, noise, slope, soil, neighbours' rights to
    light, local materials and history. The best buildings answer their site so
    specifically — the sun angle, the view corridor, the prevailing breeze — that
    transplanting them elsewhere is incoherent; a design that could stand anywhere
    usually belongs nowhere. And the process has a shape (RIBA's plan of work, the
    US's schematic → design-development → construction-documents) that exists because
    errors get ten times more expensive to fix at each stage: the cheapest drawing is
    the first one, and the program is where most of a building's quality is quietly
    decided.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> architecture is mainly about how buildings look.
  The external image is maybe a tenth of the work; the rest is making a structure that
  is safe, a climate envelope that is comfortable, a plan that moves people and goods
  with minimum friction, and services (air, water, power, data) that arrive where
  needed. The most influential architectural decisions — orientation, structural grid,
  floor-to-floor height, core placement — are nearly invisible from the street, and
  nearly impossible to change later.</p>

  <h2>The services layer: the building's organs</h2>
  <p>
    Much of utilitas is invisible in photographs: the <strong>building services</strong>
    (mechanical, electrical, plumbing — "MEP") that circulate air, water, power and
    data. Ventilation is specified in litres per second per person (roughly 8–10 l/s
    for offices — the CO<sub>2</sub> arithmetic from above, made contractual); hot
    water arrives at controlled temperatures both for comfort and legionella control;
    electrical distribution, lighting levels (measured in lux — roughly 300–500 lux
    for offices, higher for detailed work) and data cabling are routed through
    ceiling voids and risers that the structural grid must reserve space for. In
    modern buildings the services cost approaches or exceeds the structure's —
    hospitals are machines with rooms attached in a literal budgetary sense — and
    the architect's coordination job is spatial: giving every duct, pipe and cable
    a continuous path from plant room to outlet without colliding with structure
    or swallowing floor-to-floor height. The plans-and-sections lesson's clash
    detection exists for exactly this layer. Bad services are the classic hidden
    failure: a building can look finished and be unbearable — stuffy, dark,
    noisy — because the organs were value-engineered out.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Vitruvius in context.</strong> <em>De architectura</em> (c. 25–15 bce) was
    written by a military engineer for Augustus, and its three-legged test — with its
    claim that building requires both hand and reason — was rediscovered in the
    Renaissance and became the profession's founding charter. What the textbook
    compresses: Vitruvius also wrote on acoustics (theatre resonance vessels), machine
    design and aqueduct surveying; "architecture" was then one discipline from the
    foundation trench to the fresco. The modern splitting of that unity into
    architect/engineer/services-consultant is itself a design question the profession
    keeps re-arguing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Post-occupancy evaluation.</strong> The discipline's honesty mechanism:
    measure buildings after people move in — energy use against prediction, comfort
    surveys, air quality, how spaces are actually used (the gap between the atrium
    imagined and the shortcut worn across the grass is a classic). Studies repeatedly
    find designed energy performance overshooting measured use by 2–3× (the
    <em>performance gap</em>: untested details, poor commissioning, occupant behaviour),
    which is why the best practices now treat commissioning and in-use feedback as part
    of design rather than someone else's invoice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How much of "delight" can be measured — eye-tracking,
    EEG and walkability metrics are edging into practice, with the obvious risk of
    optimising what is easy to count. How buildings can become material banks
    (design-for-disassembly, passports for components) rather than future rubble. And
    the welfare leg's frontier: housing as a determinant of health, where the evidence
    (cold, damp and mould against respiratory outcomes; instability against child
    development) says the built environment's largest health effects sit in the ordinary
    housing stock, not the landmarks — architecture's biggest patient rarely makes the
    photographs.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Vitruvius, De architectura (c. 25 bce); Ulrich (1984), Science; Newman, Defensible Space (1972); UNEP Global Status Report for Buildings and Construction; RIBA Plan of Work; the statics, thermodynamics and design lessons of this atlas.</p>

